<p id="p2">You think faster than you can type. Across a day of email, Slack, notes, and chatbot prompts, that gap quietly eats your time.</p>
<p id="p3"><a href="https://cotypist.app/macstories" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cotypist</a> closes that gap. It’s smart autocomplete for your Mac: as you write, it predicts your next few words and shows them inline, right at your cursor. Press Tab to take the next word or the whole line, then carry on. If a suggestion isn’t what you had in mind, keep typing and it catches up within a letter or two. Accept a word here and a line there, and you’ll have to type far less than you used to.</p>
<p id="p4">Most AI writing tools try to do too much and don’t sound like you. Cotypist only fills in the words you’d have typed anyway (just faster), and it picks up your vocabulary as you go, so the result still sounds like you. It works almost everywhere you write: Mail, Messages, Slack, Notes, Obsidian, your browser, even the prompt box in ChatGPT. And it all runs on your Mac, so nothing you type ever leaves your Mac.</p>
<p id="p5">Cotypist comes from the maker of Timing, the Mac time-tracker many MacStories readers already know, so it’s built to feel native and stay out of your way. Setup takes about a minute: drag it into Applications, launch it, then start typing, no account required.</p>
<p id="p6">Cotypist is free to download, with a 30-day Pro trial and no credit card. When the trial ends, you can keep Pro or drop to the free plan. Until the end of August, MacStories readers get 10% off their first bill at <a href="https://cotypist.app/macstories" rel="noopener noreferrer">cotypist.app/macstories</a>. Give it ten minutes and see if it can keep up with you.</p>
